WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.730
Think about the last time you grew at work. Not

00:00:02.730 --> 00:00:05.461
a training you attended, but a time you came out

00:00:05.461 --> 00:00:08.191
able to do something you could not do before. Chances

00:00:08.191 --> 00:00:10.922
are it involved real work, some stretch, and someone who

00:00:10.922 --> 00:00:12.560
told you how it was going.

00:00:12.910 --> 00:00:16.390
That pattern has research behind it. A study of 60

00:00:16.390 --> 00:00:19.870
managers tracked 225 work experiences. Skill growth rose with challenge,

00:00:19.870 --> 00:00:23.350
but it leveled off when the challenge got very steep.

00:00:23.350 --> 00:00:26.830
What kept people learning at the hardest end was feedback.

00:00:27.180 --> 00:00:30.732
You may have heard that expertise comes from thousands of

00:00:30.732 --> 00:00:34.283
hours of practice. A large meta-analysis tested that claim. In

00:00:34.283 --> 00:00:37.835
games, music and sports, deliberate practice explained a real share

00:00:37.835 --> 00:00:41.386
of the differences between people. In professions, it explained less

00:00:41.386 --> 00:00:44.938
than one percent. A careful repeat of the famous violin

00:00:44.938 --> 00:00:48.489
study found a smaller effect than the original. Practice helps.

00:00:48.489 --> 00:00:50.620
It is not the whole story.

00:00:50.970 --> 00:00:54.330
So what else matters? One answer is what you do

00:00:54.330 --> 00:00:57.690
with experience after it happens. Across 46 samples, debriefs improved

00:00:57.690 --> 00:01:01.050
performance by about a quarter on average. And they worked

00:01:01.050 --> 00:01:04.410
for individuals, not just teams. You can debrief yourself in

00:01:04.410 --> 00:01:07.770
five minutes. What did I intend? What happened? Why? What

00:01:07.770 --> 00:01:09.450
will I do next time?

00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:13.283
Include what went well. In a study of 98 soldiers

00:01:13.283 --> 00:01:16.767
on navigation exercises, those who reviewed both successes and failures

00:01:16.767 --> 00:01:20.250
improved more than those who reviewed only failures. Most people

00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:23.733
learn from mistakes by habit. Few ask why something worked,

00:01:23.733 --> 00:01:26.520
so they cannot do it again on purpose.

00:01:26.870 --> 00:01:30.322
Then there is initiative. A two-year study of 180 employees

00:01:30.322 --> 00:01:33.774
asked what proactive people actually do. Three things stood out.

00:01:33.774 --> 00:01:37.226
They tried new approaches. They learned how decisions get made

00:01:37.226 --> 00:01:40.677
and who shapes them. And they made their goals known

00:01:40.677 --> 00:01:44.129
and sought out development. All three were linked to later

00:01:44.129 --> 00:01:45.510
career progress and satisfaction.

00:01:45.860 --> 00:01:49.493
Be realistic about the payoff. Across many studies, proactive personality

00:01:49.493 --> 00:01:53.127
has only a modest link to salary and promotion. The

00:01:53.127 --> 00:01:56.760
link to career satisfaction is stronger. In state service, where

00:01:56.760 --> 00:02:00.393
advancement follows job classes and openings, satisfaction may be the

00:02:00.393 --> 00:02:03.300
most reliable reward. It is a real one.

00:02:03.650 --> 00:02:07.113
One last finding is worth carrying into your next conversation

00:02:07.113 --> 00:02:10.575
with a supervisor. In a study of 264 professionals, support

00:02:10.575 --> 00:02:14.038
for development predicted better performance when people saw a career

00:02:14.038 --> 00:02:17.501
path. When they did not, that same support predicted more

00:02:17.501 --> 00:02:20.964
leaving. So when you ask for training or a stretch

00:02:20.964 --> 00:02:24.426
assignment, connect it to something you can see ahead: a

00:02:24.426 --> 00:02:26.850
role, a kind of work, a direction.

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:30.400
Here is the practice. Choose one skill. Find one piece

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:33.600
of real work that stretches it. Ask two people for

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:36.800
one specific piece of feedback. Review your work afterward, including

00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:40.000
what went right. And when support is offered, ask which

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:42.880
path it serves. Your development is yours to lead.
